imagine we have simple LOOP
the execution will depend on size of data!
the number of operations depend on number of data.


for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
          System.out.println(numbers[i]);   //the runtime complexity is O(n)
    }
 }

Note: the cost of this algorithm grows "LINEARLY"
      and in direct correlation to size of the input.
      (n is size of input)

----------------------------------

public void log(int[] numbers) {            //the runtime complexity is O(n)

    System.out.println("1");
        for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
            System.out.println(numbers[i]);
        }
        System.out.println("2");
    }

Note: the constant will remove,
      it is still linear method
      O(1+n+1) = O(n)

